    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "By considering $\\left(\\sqrt{x}-\\dfrac1{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)$, prove that ‘the sum of a positive number and its reciprocal is greater than or equal to 2’.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "For $x>0$, $\\left(\\sqrt{x}-\\dfrac1{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)^2=x-2+\\dfrac1x\\geq0$, so $x+\\dfrac1x\\geq2$.",
      "worked": "For $x>0$, the square roots and reciprocals exist, and every real square is non-negative.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac1{\\sqrt{x}}\\right)^2&\\geq0\\\\x-2+\\frac1x&\\geq0\\\\x+\\frac1x&\\geq2.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
